---
id: WC-002
type: rule
family: weak-cryptography
title: Broken cipher or ECB mode in a construction call
version: 1

severity: error
confidence: high
spectrum_position: 2

targets: [source]
file_extensions: [.ts, .tsx, .js, .jsx, .mjs, .cjs, .py, .java]
allowlist:
  [
    '**/*.test.ts',
    '**/*.test.tsx',
    '**/*.test.js',
    '**/*.test.jsx',
    '**/*.spec.ts',
    '**/*.spec.tsx',
    '**/*.spec.js',
    '**/*.spec.jsx',
    '**/__tests__/**',
    '**/test_*.py',
    '**/*_test.py',
    '**/tests/**',
    '**/explain/**',
  ]

detection:
  type: regex
  pattern: "create(?:de)?cipher(?:iv)?\\(\\s*['\"](?:des|desede|rc4|[a-z0-9-]*ecb)[^'\"]*['\"]|Cipher\\.getInstance\\(\\s*['\"](?:des|rc4|[^'\"]*ecb)[^'\"]*['\"]|\\bMODE_ECB\\b|\\b(?:DES|ARC4)\\.new\\s*\\("
  flags: i

related: [WC-001]
enabled: true
opt_in: false
---

You're constructing a cipher with a broken primitive or an insecure
mode. DES has a brute-forceable 56-bit key, RC4 has biased keystream
output, and ECB mode encrypts identical plaintext blocks to identical
ciphertext — so structure in the plaintext survives encryption. None of
these give you the confidentiality the call implies.

Use an authenticated mode with a modern cipher: AES-256-GCM or
ChaCha20-Poly1305, with a unique nonce per message
(`createCipheriv('aes-256-gcm', key, iv)`, `AES.new(key, AES.MODE_GCM)`,
`Cipher.getInstance("AES/GCM/NoPadding")`). Authentication (the GCM/Poly1305
tag) is not optional — it's what stops an attacker tampering with
ciphertext.

If this is a compatibility shim talking to a frozen legacy peer that
only speaks the old primitive, isolate it and suppress WC-002 with a
reason explaining the interop constraint.
